.TH DWIM 1 2026-11-01 "dwim 0.0.8" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
dwim \- a coding agent
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B dwim
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.RI [ PROMPT ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B dwim
is a coding agent that runs in your terminal.
It reads and edits files, runs commands, and works through a task with you in
an interactive shell.
.PP
With no arguments,
.B dwim
starts an interactive session in the current directory.
If
.I PROMPT
is given,
.B dwim
answers it without the shell and exits: the reply goes to standard output and
nothing else does, while progress, the model's thinking, the commands it runs,
and their output go to standard error.
As no one is there to answer the model, it runs in auto mode: a reply
with no command that does not end with
.B DONE
on a line of its own, and the account the model gives after a hundred
tool calls, are answered with word to go on, with the tools on, up to ten
times in a turn.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-model " model"
Model to run (default:
.BR bonsai\-2\-27b ).
.TP
.BI \-\-device " device"
Device to run the model on:
.B gpu
(default), or
.BR cpu .
.TP
.BI \-\-context " tokens"
Maximum conversation length in tokens.
Cannot exceed the model's supported context length.
Larger values reserve more memory on the selected device at startup.
.B \-\-help
shows the default on this machine, and
.B dwim
says how many tokens it gives the model, and how much memory that takes,
before it loads it.
By default, as many as fit in the memory the device has free besides the
model and 256 MB to spare, in thousands, and no fewer than 4096:
a GPU's free memory as its driver reports it, which for a GPU that shares
the host's memory is no more than the host has available; on a Mac, as
much as Metal recommends the GPU use, and no more than the host has
available; and on the CPU, the host's available memory.
Where the device cannot tell, 16384.
The conversation is compacted before it outgrows the window: when a
message or a command's output would take it past four fifths, the agent
writes itself a note of the task, what it found, what it did and what is
next, starts over from the system prompt, and reads back the note, your
earlier messages, and the most recent exchanges without their thoughts.
A reply that runs out of room is stopped short and goes on after a
compaction.
.TP
.B \-\-bench
Instead of a session, time the model at several depths of context and
print a table of tokens a second: at each of 1k, 8k, 32k, 64k and 128k
tokens of context, as many of them as the context has room for, the rate
it reads a page of 2048 tokens, and the rate it generates 32 tokens one
at a time. The context is filled with text to each depth in turn, which
takes as long as reading that much, and a row is printed as each depth
is done.
.B \-\-context
bounds the depths timed.
.TP
.BI \-\-shell " program"
Run the model's shell commands through
.I program
instead of
.BR sh ,
as
.I program
.B \-c
.IR command :
a script that runs them somewhere else, such as in a container that has
what the project needs installed, while the model, and its reading and
editing of files, stay here.
.TP
.B \-\-stats
When answering a prompt given on the command line, print where the time
went once the reply ends: the time loading the model; the tokens read as
prompts, generated while thinking, and generated as the reply, with the
time and rate of each; and the tools run.
.TP
.B \-\-thinking
Show the model's thinking in the shell from the start, as
.B Ctrl\-O
does.
.TP
.BI \-\-thinking\-budget " tokens"
Most tokens the model may think at a time before it is made to act, or 0
for no limit (default: 2048).
The model thinks before every reply, and tends to think at length; a
thought that repeats itself is ended whatever the budget. A thought that runs to
the budget is ended with a sentence in the model's own voice, as the
model's makers end one, and the model goes on to run a command or reply
from what it has thought so far.
A turn ends when the model replies with text alone, or, after a hundred
tool calls without a reply, when it is asked to say what it has done and
what remains.
After twenty calls in a row that change no file, a call's result ends with
a reminder to make the change it has found and check it, or to say what it
still needs to find out.
.TP
.BI \-\-reasoning\-effort " effort"
How much the model reasons before it replies:
.BR xhigh ,
.B medium
(default), or
.BR low .
The model's chat template asks for it with a line at the start of the
system prompt, for
.B xhigh
and
.B low
only.
The model's makers advise
.B medium
for the model's long reasoning, and find that
.B low
thinks about as long as
.BR xhigh .
.TP
.BI \-\-temperature " t"
Sample at temperature
.I t
instead of the one the model file recommends; 0 always picks the
likeliest token.
.TP
.BI \-\-transcript " file"
Write the session's transcript to
.I file
instead of a new file under
.IR ~/.local/state/dwim/projects/ .
.B /dev/null
keeps none.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Display usage information.
.SH INTERACTIVE SHELL
The shell reads one message at a time.
Anything you type is sent to the agent.
While the model is busy, the line above the input box says what it is
doing. The line under the box names the directory the agent works in.
Lines starting with a slash are commands:
.TP
.B /model
Show the model, the device it runs on, where its weights are kept, and
how many tokens of conversation it has room for, along with the other
models
.B dwim
knows.
.TP
.B /stats
Show where the session's time has gone, as
.B \-\-stats
does.
.TP
.B /transcript
Show where the session's transcript is.
.TP
.B /compact
Compact the conversation now, as happens on its own when it nears the
end of the context window.
.TP
.BI /thinking\-budget " tokens"
Set the most tokens the model may think before it is made to act, as
.B \-\-thinking\-budget
does, with 0 for no limit; alone, show it.
.TP
.BR /auto " on | off"
Turn auto mode on or off; alone, show which it is.
In it, the model goes on by itself as it does when answering a prompt
given on the command line: a reply that does not end with
.B DONE
on a line of its own, and the account it gives after a hundred tool
calls, are answered with word to go on, up to ten times in a turn.
It is off in the shell unless turned on.
.TP
.B /help
List the commands and the keys.
.PP
The model thinks before it replies, and the shell hides the thought unless
.B Ctrl\-O
shows it: pressed while the model thinks, it prints the thought so far and
what follows, and pressed again it hides the rest.
.B Ctrl\-C
or
.B Esc
interrupts the agent while it works.
.B Ctrl\-C
or
.B Ctrl\-D
on an empty line exits
.BR dwim ,
printing where the session's transcript is, and
.B Ctrl\-C
on a line with text clears it.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B AGENTS.md
If present in the working directory, its contents are given to the agent as
project instructions.
.TP
.I ~/.cache/dwim/states/
The model's state after the system prompt, saved when a prompt is first
read and taken up by later runs with the same model and prompt in place of
reading it again. The prompt changes only with the project's
.BR AGENTS.md ,
since where the agent is, the files there, the branch and the date go with
the first message instead, and the newest four states are kept. On macOS the
directory is
.I ~/Library/Caches/dwim/states/
instead.
.TP
.I ~/.local/state/dwim/projects/
A transcript of each session, unless
.B \-\-transcript
names another file, readable by the user alone. Transcripts are kept in a
folder for each working directory, named by its path with everything but
letters and digits made a dash, such as
.IR \-home\-user\-src\-dwim/ ,
and out of the agent's sight there; each is named by when the session
started (in UTC) and the process ID, such as
.IR 2026-10-01T093012-4242.jsonl .
It is JSON Lines: a record for each thing that happens, each with its
.B type
and the
.B time
in seconds since the session started.
.B start
has the model, the directory, the context, and the system prompt;
.B user
a message, what went ahead of it, and the tokens in the context before it;
.BR thought ,
.BR text ,
and
.B note
what the model thought, replied, and wrote to go on from after a
compaction;
.B reply
the end of a reply, with the calls it made, whether it was stopped short,
the tokens it read, thought, and replied, and the tokens in the context
after it;
.B call
and
.B output
a tool call and what it returned, with the seconds it ran;
.B compacting
and
.B compacted
a compaction, with the tokens before and after; and
.B error
and
.B interrupted
a turn that ended early.
Each record is written as it happens, and a thought, text, or note as it
streams, at least once a second, so a session that is killed leaves its
transcript up to then. Transcripts are not removed. On macOS the
directory is
.I ~/Library/Application Support/dwim/projects/
instead.
.TP
.I $TMPDIR/dwim-<pid>-<n>/
Where the whole output of a command that printed more than the model is
shown is kept, readable by the user alone, as a file per stream named by the
command's number, such as
.IR 3.stdout .
The result the model sees names the file. The directory is removed when
.B dwim
exits, and any left by an earlier
.B dwim
that is no longer running are removed by the next one to start.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
An error occurred.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR claude (1),
.BR codex (1),
.BR opencode (1)
